-- title:
--   Stalks of a base change along a surjection of rings
-- statement:
--   Let $q\colon O \to \kappa$ be a homomorphism of commutative rings which is surjective as a function, let $X$ and $Y$ be schemes, and let $f\colon X \to \operatorname{Spec} O$, $g\colon Y \to X$ and $t\colon Y \to \operatorname{Spec} \kappa$ be morphisms of schemes such that the square with top edge $g$, left edge $t$, right edge $f$ and bottom edge $\operatorname{Spec}(q)\colon \operatorname{Spec}\kappa \to \operatorname{Spec} O$ is a pullback square, i.e. $g$ followed by $f$ equals $t$ followed by $\operatorname{Spec}(q)$ and the resulting cone exhibits $Y$ as a fibre product. Then for every point $y$ of $Y$ two things hold. First, the induced homomorphism of local rings $\mathcal{O}_{X, g(y)} \to \mathcal{O}_{Y, y}$ on stalks, taken as a map of underlying rings, is surjective. Second, its kernel is the ideal of $\mathcal{O}_{X, g(y)}$ generated by the image of $\ker q$ under the composite ring homomorphism $O \to \Gamma(X, \mathcal{O}_X) \to \mathcal{O}_{X, g(y)}$, in which the first map is the global-sections map of $f$ precomposed with the inverse of the canonical isomorphism $\Gamma(\operatorname{Spec} O, \mathcal{O}) \cong O$, and the second is the germ map at $g(y)$ from sections over the whole of $X$. Equivalently, $\mathcal{O}_{Y,y}$ is the quotient of $\mathcal{O}_{X, g(y)}$ by the ideal generated by $\ker q$.
--
--   This is the standard description of the stalks of a closed fibre: $\operatorname{Spec}(q)$ is a closed immersion, so its base change $g$ is one as well, and the kernel of the induced map on stalks is computed from the extension of $\ker q$. The pullback square is left arbitrary so that any presentation of the fibre may be used; the result is applied in the study of local rings of curves over a discrete valuation ring, where $q$ is the residue map, and in the criterion [`AlgebraicGeometry.isIso_of_isPullback_of_isIso_of_isNilpotent_ker`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.isIso_of_isPullback_of_isIso_of_isNilpotent_ker). The proof cites [`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Hom.map_germ_ker_ideal_eq_ker_stalkMap`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Hom.map_germ_ker_ideal_eq_ker_stalkMap), which identifies the kernel of the stalk map of a closed immersion with the image of its ideal of definition on an affine open neighbourhood.
